A compiler test oracle decides which outcomes on a particular program are permitted. A second compiler can be a practical comparison partner, but it is not automatically an oracle: two translators can agree on the wrong result, and two correct translators can disagree where the language allows choices. The main task is to make the observations and admissible inputs precise enough that a mismatch is informative.

\section{Choose an Observation and a Reference}

For a deterministic, pure IR function, compare the returned value for each input. For effectful programs, compare those observable events whose order and contents are specified: a return value match will not detect a dropped write. Distinguish a compile-time rejection, a run-time failure, and an incorrect result. Pin the target ABI, language mode, compiler flags, and runtime assumptions. In a floating-point test, pin or check rounding mode and account for contraction and excess precision; an equality may not be part of the contract under all allowed settings. Boldo, Jourdan, Leroy, and Melquiond's \emph{A Formally-Verified C Compiler Supporting Floating-Point Arithmetic} highlights these semantic issues and discusses Yang and colleagues' differential random testing of C compilers.

Generate programs inside a defined, deterministic subset when the oracle is exact output equality. Avoid reading uninitialized values, relying on unspecified evaluation order, or using signed overflow as though it wrapped. Treat implementation-defined behavior as a parameter to the oracle, not unexpected noise. An independent interpreter implementing the same subset is often a better reference than another optimization level of the tested compiler, because multiple levels may share a faulty front end. To test a diagnostic or a language feature with several valid outcomes, specify the acceptable class of outcomes instead of demanding identical output strings.

\section{A Modular-Arithmetic Oracle with a Negative Control}

Suppose the test program accepts two values of type \texttt{unsigned int} and returns \texttt{(a + b) - b}, using no globals or calls. With $M=\texttt{UINT\_MAX}+1$ in the mathematical model, both arithmetic operations are modulo $M$. Thus $\bigl((a+b)\bmod M-b\bigr)\bmod M=a$ for all representable inputs. The harness can use the original value of $a$ as the expected output; it need not use the same expression as the compiled function. Test $a=M-1$, $b=1$ to force the intermediate sum to wrap, and $a=0$, $b=0$ as a zero case. No assumption about the width beyond the target's declared type is necessary.

For a negative control, replace the final subtraction by addition. With $a=M-1$, $b=1$ the mutant returns $1$ instead of $M-1$, while the all-zero case still passes. This tests the sensitivity of the harness, not the correctness of any specific compiler pass. Conversely, changing \texttt{a + b} to \texttt{b + a} is an equivalent mutation for these pure inputs, so failing to distinguish it is correct. A. Jefferson Offutt and W. Michael Craft's \emph{Using Compiler Optimization Techniques to Detect Equivalent Mutants} explains why equivalent mutants should not be counted as missed errors. An independently justified invariant can both predict an outcome and tell us which mutations are useful negative controls.

\section{Triage, Minimize, and Keep the Evidence}

The dossier's \emph{Semantic Fingerprints and Metamorphic Compiler Testing} generalizes this idea when absolute expected output is expensive: apply a transformation with a known observational relation and compare the resulting runs. For example, permutation preserves a pure modular sum, but does not necessarily preserve a trace that reports each operand as it is read. A fingerprint of normalized observations helps group failures, not prove equivalence; keep the original events and normalization rules for review. Munley, Jarmusch, and Chandrasekaran's \emph{LLM4VV} studies generating OpenACC validation tests and analyzing their outcomes. Generation can improve variety, but plausible-looking source and successful compilation are not independent checks of the assertions the test is meant to exercise.

On disagreement, save the seed, source and concrete inputs, compiler versions and options, observed results, and the oracle's premises. A reducer may remove code only while preserving a discrepancy under those premises: otherwise it can turn a valid miscompilation report into an undefined program or change the feature being tested. Replay the reduced case under the reference and the original toolchains, then retain it as a regression. If useful, submit the reduced transformation to a scoped translation validation check (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-219}). Neither agreement on the regression corpus nor a high mutation score proves that all future translations are correct.
